\title{Super-Additive Degradation in Compressed Attention:\\
An Exact Theory of the Quantization--Sparsity Interaction}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{September 1, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Two of the most widely deployed inference-time compression techniques for transformer language models --- low-bit weight quantization and top-$k$ sparse attention --- are routinely budgeted as if their accuracy costs were additive. We show that they are not. In a controlled evaluation, 4-bit group-128 weight quantization alone cost $9.19\%$ of retained accuracy and top-$16$ attention alone cost $2.32\%$, while the two combined cost $14.02\%$ rather than the additive prediction of $11.51\%$; the residual $2.51$ points is an \emph{interaction cost} that decays monotonically with the attention budget ($2.51\%$, $1.77\%$, $1.60\%$ at $k=16,20,24$).

We develop an exact theory of this interaction in an idealized averaging model of an attention head. With $\varepsilon$-bounded quantization error the interaction cost is at most $\varepsilon$, and for globally centred error at most $\varepsilon\min(1,(n-k)/k)$, a bound attained whenever $2k\le n$ and antitone in $k$. In mean square, under centred, pairwise-uncorrelated error of variance $\sigma^2$, the interaction is \emph{exactly} $\sigma^{2}(1/k-1/n)$, strictly positive for every $k<n$ and realized by the $2^{n}$-point Rademacher dither ensemble. Top-$k$ selection is stable precisely above score margin $2\varepsilon$, and the constant $2$ is sharp. The effect survives arbitrary softmax patterns: a normalized pattern supported on $k$ keys transmits $\sigma^{2}\sum_i w_i^{2}\ge\sigma^{2}/k$. Under group-correlated error with intra-group correlation $\rho$, a $k$-sparse head transmits $(\sigma^{2}k+\rho\sigma^{2}P(S))/k^{2}$, where $P(S)=\sum_t m_t(m_t-1)$ depends on the selection only through its group occupancy profile; $P(S)\le k(k-1)$ with equality exactly for group-aligned selections and $P(S)=0$ for group-spread ones, so at $\rho=1$ an aligned selection transmits the \emph{entire} variance $\sigma^{2}$. Finally, recentering the quantization error on the selected set annihilates the interaction. We also delimit the claim: explicit error patterns make the interaction negative pointwise, so the verdict is a worst-case and mean-square statement.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The additive budget assumption}

Deploying a large transformer under a memory constraint means composing compression techniques. Two dominate practice: \emph{weight quantization}, which stores each weight on a low-bit grid with one shared scale per block (typically 128 weights), and \emph{sparse attention}, which restricts each query to the $k$ highest-scoring keys so that the key--value cache holds $k$ entries rather than the full context length $n$. Because the two techniques touch different parts of the computation, practice budgets their accuracy costs additively. This paper shows the assumption is false in a quantifiable and mechanistically explicable way.

\subsection{The measurement}

A controlled integration test evaluated a transformer language model under eight arms, reporting retained accuracy relative to full precision together with cross-entropy.

