\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
arm & retained & CE \\
\midrule
full fp32 & 1.0000 & 2.697 \\
top-$k$ attention, $k=16$ & 0.9768 & 2.774 \\
top-$k$ attention, $k=20$ & 0.9803 & 2.755 \\
top-$k$ attention, $k=24$ & 0.9851 & 2.742 \\
4-bit group-128 quantization & 0.9081 & 3.015 \\
quantization $+$ $k=16$ & 0.8598 & 3.220 \\
quantization $+$ $k=20$ & 0.8707 & 3.180 \\
quantization $+$ $k=24$ & 0.8772 & 3.155 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Writing $L=1-\text{retained}$, the measured interaction at budget $k$ is $I_k=L_{\text{both}}(k)-L_{\text{attn}}(k)-L_{\text{quant}}$, giving $I_{16}=2.51\%$, $I_{20}=1.77\%$, $I_{24}=1.60\%$: positive at every budget and monotonically decaying. Three hypotheses were under test --- sub-additivity (P1), super-additivity (P2) and independence (P3). The data refute P1 and P3; the theory below refutes them as theorems and computes the exact size of the effect.

\subsection{The mechanism}

Quantization error is approximately symmetric, so a dense average over $n$ keys cancels most of it while a sparse average over $k\ll n$ keys cancels far less. Sparsification is a \emph{noise amplifier}, by a factor $n/k$ in transmitted energy. A second, discrete mechanism also operates: quantized keys project differently, so the top-$k$ threshold may select a different set of keys.

\section{The averaging model}

Fix a context of $n$ keys indexed by $\{1,\dots,n\}$. An attention head reads real values $v$. (Values are vectors in practice; all statements apply coordinatewise.)

\begin{definition}[Restricted average]
For an index set $S$ and $f:\{1,\dots,n\}\to\mathbb{R}$, $\avg_S f=\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{i\in S}f(i)$, with $\avg_\emptyset f=0$. Write $\bar f=\avg_{\{1,\dots,n\}}f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Degradations]
Let $S$ with $|S|=k$ be the selected key set and $\eta$ the quantization error added to the values. Set
\[
\degA(v,S)=|\avg_S v-\bar v|,\qquad
\degQ(\eta)=|\bar\eta|,\qquad
\degAQ(v,\eta,S)=|\avg_S(v+\eta)-\bar v|.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Interaction cost]
$I(v,\eta,S)=\degAQ(v,\eta,S)-\degA(v,S)-\degQ(\eta)$. Then $I=0$ is exact additivity (P3), $I\le0$ sub-additivity (P1), $I>0$ super-additivity (P2).
\end{definition}

The decisive structural fact is linearity of the restricted average, $\avg_S(f+g)=\avg_S f+\avg_S g$, whence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:split}
\avg_S(v+\eta)-\bar v=\underbrace{(\avg_S v-\bar v)}_{\text{sparsity bias}}+\underbrace{\avg_S\eta}_{\text{sparse noise read}} .
\end{equation}
The dense arm sees $\bar\eta$, the combined arm sees $\avg_S\eta$; the difference between these two readings of the same error field is the interaction.

\begin{lemma}[Bounded averages]\label{lem:avgbound}
If $|f(i)|\le\varepsilon$ for all $i$ and $S\ne\emptyset$, then $|\avg_S f|\le\varepsilon$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Triangle inequality on the sum, then divide by $|S|>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bounded interaction penalty]
If $|\eta_i|\le\varepsilon$ for all $i$ and $S\ne\emptyset$, then $I(v,\eta,S)\le\varepsilon$ for every $v$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:split} and the triangle inequality, $\degAQ\le\degA+|\avg_S\eta|\le\degA+\varepsilon$ using Lemma~\ref{lem:avgbound}; and $\degQ\ge0$.
\end{proof}

Budget tables therefore need a correction, but a bounded one, on the scale of the quantization step. The bound holds for \emph{every} selected set, hence also across selection flips.

\section{The interaction is real}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:degQzero}
If $\sum_i\eta_i=0$ then $\degQ(\eta)=0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Exact interaction identity]
Suppose $\sum_i\eta_i=0$ and $(\avg_S v-\bar v)\cdot\avg_S\eta\ge0$. Then $I(v,\eta,S)=|\avg_S\eta|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:degQzero}, $\degQ=0$. By \eqref{eq:split}, $\degAQ=|a+b|$ with $a=\avg_S v-\bar v$, $b=\avg_S\eta$. When $ab\ge0$ one has $|a+b|=|a|+|b|$ (four sign cases; opposite signs force a factor to vanish). Hence $\degAQ=\degA+|b|$ and $I=|b|$.
\end{proof}

Dense attention averages the error away; sparse attention cannot. The additive budget predicts $\degA+0$; the truth is $\degA+|\avg_S\eta|$.

\begin{example}[Four keys]\label{ex:four}
Let $n=4$, $v=(0,0,3,3)$, $\eta=(-1,-1,-1,+1)$, $S=\{1,2\}$. Then $\bar v=3/2$, $\avg_S v=0$, $\degA=3/2$; $\bar\eta=-1/2$, $\degQ=1/2$; $\avg_S\eta=-1$, $\degAQ=|0-1-3/2|=5/2$. Hence $I=\tfrac52-\tfrac32-\tfrac12=\tfrac12>0$.
\end{example}

\begin{corollary}[P1 and P3 refuted]
There is no universal sub-additive law: $\degAQ\le\degA+\degQ$ fails. Nor are the axes independent: $\degAQ\ne\degA+\degQ$ in general.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided budget law]
For $\varepsilon$-bounded, globally centred quantization error and nonempty $S$ with $|S|=k$,
\[
\degAQ\;\le\;\degA+\degQ+\varepsilon\min\!\Big(1,\tfrac{n-k}{k}\Big),
\]
while Example~\ref{ex:four} shows the naive additive expression can be strictly exceeded. Additivity is a \emph{lower} bound on damage, never an upper bound.
\end{theorem}

\section{The exact worst case}

Define $W(n,k,\varepsilon)=\varepsilon\min\!\big(1,(n-k)/k\big)$.

\begin{theorem}[Worst case, upper half]\label{thm:wc}
If $\sum_i\eta_i=0$, $|\eta_i|\le\varepsilon$ for all $i$, and $S\ne\emptyset$ with $|S|=k$, then $|\avg_S\eta|\le W(n,k,\varepsilon)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Two bounds. Lemma~\ref{lem:avgbound} gives $|\avg_S\eta|\le\varepsilon$. Global centring gives $\sum_{i\in S}\eta_i=-\sum_{i\notin S}\eta_i$, whose modulus is at most $(n-k)\varepsilon$, so $|\avg_S\eta|\le\varepsilon(n-k)/k$. Take the minimum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Achievability]
If $2k\le n$, $\varepsilon\ge0$ and $S\ne\emptyset$, there is $\eta$ with $\sum_i\eta_i=0$, $|\eta_i|\le\varepsilon$, and $\avg_S\eta=\varepsilon$; moreover $W(n,k,\varepsilon)=\varepsilon$ in this regime.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $\eta_i=\varepsilon$ on $S$ and $\eta_i=-\frac{k}{n-k}\varepsilon$ off $S$. The sum vanishes; $2k\le n$ gives $k\le n-k$, hence $|\eta_i|\le\varepsilon$ off $S$; and $\avg_S\eta=\varepsilon$. Also $(n-k)/k\ge1$, so $W=\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

Thus in the practically relevant regime an entire quantization step can hide inside a sparse window.

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity in the budget]
For $\varepsilon\ge0$ and $k_1\le k_2$, $W(n,k_2,\varepsilon)\le W(n,k_1,\varepsilon)$; if $\varepsilon>0$ and $n/2\le k_1<k_2<n$ the inequality is strict.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$k\mapsto n/k-1$ is strictly decreasing, and $\min(1,\cdot)$ preserves monotonicity; for $k\ge n/2$ the minimum is the second argument.
\end{proof}

This is the shape of the measurement: $I_{16}>I_{20}>I_{24}$.

\section{Mechanism (1): the moving top-$k$ threshold}

\begin{definition}
$S$ is a \emph{top-$k$ set} for a score vector $s$ if $|S|=k$ and $s_j\le s_i$ for all $i\in S$, $j\notin S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Selection stability]\label{thm:stab}
Let $S$ be a top-$k$ set for $s$ and $S'$ a top-$k$ set for $s+e$ with $|e_i|\le\varepsilon$. If $s_j+2\varepsilon<s_i$ for all $i\in S$, $j\notin S$, then $S'=S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $S'\ne S$ then, as $|S|=|S'|$, both $S'\setminus S$ and $S\setminus S'$ are nonempty; pick $j\in S'\setminus S$, $i\in S\setminus S'$. Optimality of $S'$ gives $s_i+e_i\le s_j+e_j$, so $s_i\le s_j+2\varepsilon$, contradicting the margin.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of the constant $2$]
There exist scores, perturbations and top-$1$ sets with $|e_i|\le\varepsilon$, margin exactly $2\varepsilon$, and $S'\ne S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$n=2$, $s=(1,-1)$, $e=(-1,+1)$, $\varepsilon=1$: $S=\{1\}$ is top-$1$ for $s$ with margin $2=2\varepsilon$, and the perturbed scores $(0,0)$ admit $S'=\{2\}$.
\end{proof}

The phase boundary sits exactly at margin $2\varepsilon$: below it quantization can re-route attention, strictly above it provably cannot.

\section{Mechanism (2): noise gain}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{noise gain} of a weight vector $w$ supported on $S$ is $\mathcal{G}(w)=\sum_{i\in S}w_i^{2}$. For uncorrelated $\eta_i$ of variance $\sigma^2$, the read $\sum_i w_i\eta_i$ has variance $\sigma^{2}\mathcal{G}(w)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cauchy--Schwarz floor]\label{thm:cs}
If $S\ne\emptyset$ and $\sum_{i\in S}w_i=1$, then $\mathcal{G}(w)\ge1/|S|$, with equality for uniform weights.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$1=\big(\sum_{i\in S}w_i\big)^{2}\le|S|\sum_{i\in S}w_i^{2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Amplification by sparsification]
Restricting a normalized pattern from $n$ keys to $k$ keys multiplies the transmitted quantization energy by at least $n/k$: the floor moves from $1/n$ to $1/k$. The energy $\sigma^{2}/k$ of a uniform $k$-sparse head is strictly decreasing in $k$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The mean-square theory}

Let $\Omega$ be a finite nonempty ensemble of quantization outcomes with uniform average $\E$, and $\eta_\omega$ the error field of outcome $\omega$. Assume the \emph{dither model}: (a) $\E[\eta_\omega(i)]=0$; (b) $\E[\eta_\omega(i)\eta_\omega(j)]=0$ for $i\ne j$; (c) $\E[\eta_\omega(i)^{2}]=\sigma^{2}$.

\begin{lemma}[Second moment of a sparse read]\label{lem:sparse2}
Under (a)--(c) and $|S|=k\ge1$, $\E[\avg_S\eta_\omega]=0$ and $\E\big[(\avg_S\eta_\omega)^{2}\big]=\sigma^{2}/k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Expand $k^{-2}\sum_{i,j\in S}\eta_i\eta_j$; the $k(k-1)$ off-diagonal terms vanish by (b) and the $k$ diagonal terms equal $\sigma^{2}$ by (c).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact mean-square interaction]\label{thm:crown}
Under (a)--(c), for any $v$ and nonempty $S$ with $|S|=k$,
\[
\E\big[(\avg_S(v+\eta_\omega)-\bar v)^{2}\big]-(\avg_S v-\bar v)^{2}-\E\big[\bar\eta_\omega^{\,2}\big]
=\sigma^{2}\Big(\frac1k-\frac1n\Big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $b=\avg_S v-\bar v$. By \eqref{eq:split} the combined error is $b+\avg_S\eta_\omega$, with mean square $b^{2}+2b\,\E[\avg_S\eta_\omega]+\E[(\avg_S\eta_\omega)^{2}]$. The cross-term vanishes and the last term is $\sigma^{2}/k$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:sparse2}; the attention-only term is $b^{2}$ and the quantization-only term is $\sigma^{2}/n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $\sigma\ne0$ and $k<n$ the mean-square interaction is strictly positive. Super-additivity is structural: no adversary and no sign condition are needed.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Non-vacuity: Rademacher dither]
Let $\Omega=\{\pm1\}^{n}$ be uniform and $\eta_\omega(i)=\sigma\omega_i$. Then (a)--(c) hold, so the interaction is exactly $\sigma^{2}(1/k-1/n)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Each first-moment and cross-moment statistic is odd under flipping one coordinate sign, an involution of $\Omega$, so its average vanishes; the second moment is $\sigma^{2}$ pointwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The interaction is not pointwise positive]
On $v=(0,0,3,3)$, $S=\{1,2\}$, take $\eta'=(+1,+1,+1,-1)$. Then $\degQ=1/2$, $\avg_S\eta'=1$, $\degAQ=1/2$, so $I=-3/2<0$.
\end{proposition}

Super-additivity is therefore a worst-case and mean-square statement, not a pointwise identity.

\section{Arbitrary attention patterns}

\begin{theorem}[Transmitted variance of an arbitrary pattern]
Under (a)--(c), $\E\big[(\sum_i w_i\eta_\omega(i))^{2}\big]=\sigma^{2}\sum_i w_i^{2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Softmax-ready interaction floor]
If $w$ is supported on $S$ with $|S|=k$ and $\sum_{i\in S}w_i=1$, then
$\sigma^{2}\sum_i w_i^{2}-\sigma^{2}/n\ \ge\ \sigma^{2}(1/k-1/n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine the previous theorem with Theorem~\ref{thm:cs}.
\end{proof}

Hence the uniform top-$k$ head is the \emph{best} case among all patterns with a given $k$-element support: a peaked softmax pays strictly more.

\section{Group-correlated quantization}

Group-wise 4-bit quantization shares one scale across a block, so hypothesis (b) fails by design. Let $\grp:\{1,\dots,n\}\to G$ assign keys to groups and assume $\E[\eta_\omega(i)^{2}]=\sigma^{2}$ and, for $i\ne j$,
\[
\E[\eta_\omega(i)\eta_\omega(j)]=\begin{cases}\rho\sigma^{2}, & \grp(i)=\grp(j),\\ 0,&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]

\begin{definition}
For $i\in S$ let $p_S(i)=\#\{j\in S: j\ne i,\ \grp(j)=\grp(i)\}$, and let $P(S)=\sum_{i\in S}p_S(i)$ be the number of ordered same-group pairs in $S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact grouped mean-square read]\label{thm:grouped}
For nonempty $S$ with $|S|=k$,
\[
\E\big[(\avg_S\eta_\omega)^{2}\big]=\frac{\sigma^{2}k+\rho\sigma^{2}P(S)}{k^{2}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Expand the double sum $k^{-2}\sum_{i,j\in S}\E[\eta_i\eta_j]$. Row $i$ contributes $\sigma^{2}$ on the diagonal and $\rho\sigma^{2}$ for each of its $p_S(i)$ same-group partners, other terms vanishing.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Spread versus aligned]
If no two selected keys share a group then $P(S)=0$ and the head transmits $\sigma^{2}/k$. If all selected keys lie in one group then $P(S)=k(k-1)$ and the head transmits $\sigma^{2}\big(1+\rho(k-1)\big)/k$, strictly larger whenever $\rho>0$, $\sigma\ne0$, $k\ge2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Total loss of averaging at $\rho=1$]
Under shared-scale dither --- one uniform sign $\omega_g\in\{\pm1\}$ per group, $\eta_\omega(i)=\sigma\omega_{\grp(i)}$ --- a selection contained in a single quantization group satisfies $\E\big[(\avg_S\eta_\omega)^{2}\big]=\sigma^{2}$.
\end{theorem}

The $1/k$ suppression that makes low-bit weights survivable disappears entirely; spreading the same $k$ keys across distinct groups restores $\sigma^{2}/k$.

\subsection{The pair statistic is a group occupancy profile}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{group occupancy profile} of $S$ is $(m_t)_{t\in G}$ with $m_t=\#\{i\in S:\grp(i)=t\}$, so $\sum_t m_t=k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Profile identity]\label{thm:profile}
$P(S)=\sum_t m_t(m_t-1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Partition $S$ into the fibres of $\grp$: each of the $m_t$ elements of the fibre over $t$ has $p_S(i)=m_t-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Aligned is worst, spread is best]\label{cor:extremal}
$0\le P(S)\le k(k-1)$ for every selection and every group structure; the upper bound is attained exactly by selections inside a single group, and $P(S)=0$ exactly when no two selected keys share a group.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Variance form of the extremal statement]
For every $\rho\ge0$ and nonempty $S$ with $|S|=k$,
$\E\big[(\avg_S\eta_\omega)^{2}\big]\le\sigma^{2}\big(1+\rho(k-1)\big)/k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Insert Corollary~\ref{cor:extremal} into Theorem~\ref{thm:grouped}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Smoothing step]\label{thm:smooth}
For reals $a,b$ with $b+2\le a$,
\[
(a-1)(a-2)+(b+1)b\;<\;a(a-1)+b(b-1).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The difference of the two sides equals $2(a-b-1)>0$.
\end{proof}

Moving one selected key from a group holding $a$ of them to a group holding $b\le a-2$ therefore strictly decreases $P(S)$: the penalty is a Schur-convex function of the occupancy profile, and balanced profiles are optimal.

\begin{remark}[Design rule]
Under group-wise quantization, among selections of equal attention quality prefer the one whose group occupancy profile is most balanced. Transmitted quantization energy ranges from $\sigma^{2}/k$ (perfectly spread) up to $\sigma^{2}(1+\rho(k-1))/k$ (fully aligned).
\end{remark}

\section{Removing the interaction: selection-aware recentering}

\begin{theorem}[Recentering kills the interaction]
If $\sum_{i\in S}\eta_i=0$ then $\degAQ(v,\eta,S)=\degA(v,S)$ and $I(v,\eta,S)=-\degQ(\eta)\le0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The hypothesis gives $\avg_S\eta=0$, so by \eqref{eq:split} the combined error equals the sparsity bias exactly.
\end{proof}

Practically this argues for \emph{selection-aware calibration}: choose quantization offsets so that the residual error has zero mean over the keys the head actually attends to, rather than over the full context. Together with group diversification this gives two cheap mitigations, one per identified mechanism.

\section{Reconciling theory with measurement}

The theory predicts (i) a positive interaction --- measured $+2.51\%,+1.77\%,+1.60\%$; (ii) antitone dependence on $k$ --- measured monotone decreasing; (iii) a rate close to $1/k-1/n$ --- anchoring at $k=16$, a pure $1/k$ law predicts $2.01\%$ at $k=20$ and $1.67\%$ at $k=24$ against measured $1.77\%$ and $1.60\%$, so the observed decay is slightly faster but of the right order; and (iv) additional amplification from group correlation, consistent with the interaction being of the same order as the attention-only loss. Cross-entropy corroborates: at $k=16$ the combined excess is $0.523$ nats against an additive prediction of $0.395$ nats, a $32\%$ relative shortfall.

\section{Discussion and limitations}

The model is a scalar-valued averaging head: dimension-free and exact, but silent on several realistic effects. Depth is not modelled --- per-layer interactions compose in a way outside the present scope, whereas the measured figure is whole-network. The selected set is treated as fixed in the mean-square theory, whereas in reality it depends on the quantized scores, so the two mechanisms are coupled; Theorem~\ref{thm:stab} bounds when the coupling is inactive without integrating the two. We perturb values, while quantization also perturbs query--key products. The block-correlation model is a two-parameter idealization, and $\rho$ is not directly measurable without instrumenting the quantizer. None of this weakens the central negative result: additivity is refuted by a four-key example, and the mean-square excess is an exact identity.

\section{Future work}

(1) \emph{Global Schur-convexity}: complete the extremal picture by showing that among profiles with $\sum_t m_t=k$ and $m_t\le g$ the balanced profiles minimise $\sum_t m_t(m_t-1)$; Theorem~\ref{thm:smooth} supplies the smoothing step. (2) \emph{Softmax temperature monotonicity}: make $\sum_i w_i^{2}$ an explicit monotone function of inverse temperature, trading attention sharpness against quantization robustness. (3) \emph{Coupling the two mechanisms}: bound the total interaction when $S$ is itself chosen from perturbed scores, as an expectation over the score-gap distribution. (4) \emph{Multi-layer composition}: determine whether per-layer interactions accumulate additively, multiplicatively, or sub-linearly in depth.

\section{Conclusion}

Combining low-bit weight quantization with top-$k$ sparse attention is super-additive. In the worst case the excess is exactly $\varepsilon\min(1,(n-k)/k)$, equal to a full quantization step whenever the attention budget is at most half the context. In mean square it is exactly $\sigma^{2}(1/k-1/n)$, strictly positive for every sparse budget and growing as the budget shrinks. Peaked attention pays more, not less. Group-wise quantization can multiply the penalty by up to $k$, precisely when the selected keys concentrate in a single group --- a condition characterized completely by the occupancy profile, whose penalty $\sum_t m_t(m_t-1)$ is maximal for aligned selections and zero for spread ones. The correctives are equally precise: diversify the selection across quantization groups, and centre the quantization error on the keys the head actually reads.

